<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty · Peace</p><h1 class="enc-title">Treaty of San Francisco</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1951</span> · Also known as San Francisco Peace Treaty, Treaty of Peace with Japan · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-francisco">San Francisco</a> was signed on 8 September 1951 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> and 48 Allied nations, formally ending the state of war of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>. It took effect on 28 April 1952, ending the Allied occupation and restoring Japan's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a>, and Japan gave up its claims to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiwan">Taiwan</a> and other territories. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> did not sign, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> was not invited.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/treaty-of-san-francisco.jpg" alt="Treaty of San Francisco" width="320" height="265" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown authorUnknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Yoshida_signs_San_Francisco_Peace_Treaty.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
